    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p12-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "What is the concentration of the antibiotic after 1 hour? Give your answer to 4 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1.3357\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}C(1)&=9(e^{-0.2}-e^{-0.4})\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\\\\C(1)&\\approx1.3357\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 15
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let $x=e^{-0.2t}$ and show that when $C=2$, the resulting quadratic is $9x^2-9x+2=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Since $e^{-0.4t}=x^2$, the equation $2=9(x-x^2)$ rearranges to $9x^2-9x+2=0$.",
          "worked": "With $x=e^{-0.2t}$, we have $e^{-0.4t}=(e^{-0.2t})^2=x^2$. At $C=2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}2&=9(x-x^2)\\\\2&=9x-9x^2\\\\9x^2-9x+2&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Solve for $x$, and hence show that it will take just over 2 hours to first reach $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$. Give the times to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac{2}{3}$ or $x=\\frac{1}{3}$, giving times of about $2.03$ and $5.49$ hours respectively. The first time is just over 2 hours.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}9x^2-9x+2&=0\\\\(3x-1)(3x-2)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThe roots are $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}e^{-0.2t}&=x\\\\-0.2t&=\\ln x\\\\t&=-5\\ln x\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=\\frac{2}{3}$,\n$$\\begin{align*}t&=5\\ln\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\\\t&\\approx2.03.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=\\frac{1}{3}$,\n$$\\begin{align*}t&=5\\ln 3\\\\t&\\approx5.49.\\end{align*}$$\nThe concentration first reaches $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ after about $2.03$ hours.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 53
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q12-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "You should have obtained two solutions. Give a biological reason why there may be two times when the concentration is $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The concentration passes through $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ as the antibiotic enters the bloodstream, and again as the body breaks down and clears the antibiotic.",
          "worked": "At first, the concentration rises as the antibiotic is absorbed into the bloodstream. Later, the concentration falls as the body metabolises and clears the antibiotic. The concentration passes through $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ once on the way up and once on the way down.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      }
    ]
  },
